WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.026 --> 00:00:02.960
INTRO:
Not too long ago, NFTs were the hottest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:10.240
commodity on the market. We saw singers, athletes,&nbsp;
YouTubers, and even comedians buy into the craze.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:15.520
These guys were dropping hundreds of thousands&nbsp;
if not millions of dollars on these non-fungible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.960
tokens. And while non fungible tokens might&nbsp;
sound like some sort of fancy investment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:26.800
in the end, it’s really just a digital picture. In&nbsp;
fact, unless you told someone, they wouldn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.400
know whether you paid for the picture or not. But,&nbsp;
with that being said, at least these celebrities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:46.880
could afford it. Usually, these guys are worth&nbsp;
tens of millions if not hundreds of millions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.640
so losing a few hundred thousand or even a few&nbsp;
million wouldn’t drastically change their lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.840
But, these guys weren’t the only ones that were&nbsp;
buying into the craze. Loads of retail traders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.880
were sinking their life savings into dozens of&nbsp;
jpegs hoping that at least one of them would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:09.040
become the next crypto punk or bored ape. But, as&nbsp;
the entire market started to crash, it didn’t take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.560
long for NFTs to follow. For example, in April,&nbsp;
bored apes were selling for $400 to $500,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.680
but now, they’re selling for just over $100,000.&nbsp;
Similarly, cryptopunks have also crashed from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:28.560
over $400,000 to under $100,000. And these&nbsp;
are the most prominent and notable NFTs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.971
Many of the NFTs that everyday people were&nbsp;
buying are down over 90% from their all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.971 --> 00:01:39.200
time highs which is not that surprising given&nbsp;
that NFT sales have crashed 92% from the peak.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.000
But how did this entire craze even&nbsp;
start and will NFTs ever recover?

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.840
HISTORY OF NFTS:&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.440
Well, taking a look back, the idea of NFTs has&nbsp;
actually been around for quite some time dating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:01.360
all the way back to 2012. At the time, a man named&nbsp;
Meni Rosenfield published a whitepaper called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.000
Colored Coins. The paper described a method in&nbsp;
which real world assets could be represented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.160
and accounted for in the digital world.&nbsp;
The idea was to create tokens that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.400
similar to bitcoins in that transactions&nbsp;
would be accounted for on the blockchain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.440
But, the difference was that each token would&nbsp;
be unique and distinguishable unlike bitcoins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.920
While this was a rather interesting idea to many&nbsp;
crypto enthusiasts, the idea couldn’t really be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.320
implemented due to the limitations with Bitcoin&nbsp;
at the time. This didn’t stop enthusiasts from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.680
trying to make colored coins a reality though,&nbsp;
and that brings us to an artist named Kevin McCoy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:39.280
In 2014, Kevin Created a piece of digital art&nbsp;
called Quantum. Quantum was a rather simple piece,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.320
it was basically just a octagon that was pulsating&nbsp;
in different colors. But, what made it unique was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:49.360
Kevin’s efforts to make it into an NFT. Since he&nbsp;
couldn’t leverage Bitcoin, he launched Quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:55.200
on secondary blockchain called Namecoin which made&nbsp;
Quantum the world’s first NFT. I don’t think you’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.080
be surprised to hear that Quantum was sold for&nbsp;
well over a million dollars at the peak. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:04.880
after the release of Quantum, we saw a lot more&nbsp;
buzz in the crypto community surrounding NFTs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.080
There was a lot of hype surrounding native support&nbsp;
for NFTs from Bitcoin, but this never really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:16.800
happened. Even today, the Bitcoin blockchain does&nbsp;
not natively support NFTs. The problem was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:22.320
Bitcoin wasn’t built with real assets in mind, so&nbsp;
trying to inject this new idea into Bitcoin was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:28.800
rather difficult. And this is where Ethereum comes&nbsp;
in. Ethereum wasn’t launched till mid 2015, so the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.440
development team had quite a bit of time to study&nbsp;
the shortfalls of Bitcoin and address them. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:39.200
Ethereum team didn’t support NFTs directly per se,&nbsp;
but they built a robust system of smart contracts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:44.720
which made creating, minting, and distributing&nbsp;
NFTs easier than ever before. And this ease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.440
caught the attention of two software developers&nbsp;
named John Watkinson and Matt Hall who decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.400
to create their own set of NFTs. They launched&nbsp;
this experimental NFT project with 10,000 pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.680
that were inspired by London’s punk culture, and&nbsp;
they decided to call them CryptoPunks. Initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:03.760
they actually handed out CryptoPunks for&nbsp;
free to anyone with an Ethereum wallet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:10.080
This is quite ironic given that each of these&nbsp;
would eventually be worth over $400,000. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:15.040
since then, CryptoPunks have become an OG within&nbsp;
the crypto space, and we’ve seen thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.400
projects try to replicate their success, but only&nbsp;
a few have even come close such as the Bored Apes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.720
In the meantime, crypto exchanges were also hard&nbsp;
at work trying to make it as easy as possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.400
for everyday people to buy and trade NFTs and that&nbsp;
brings us into the insanity that we saw last year.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.720
LEGITAMITE USE CASES:&nbsp;

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:39.760
Hearing the history of NFTs probably didn’t&nbsp;
convince most of you guys that NFTs are useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:45.120
by any means. After all, it’s basically just a&nbsp;
couple of art collections giong viral with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:51.760
help of blockchain technology. But, digital art&nbsp;
is just one use case for NFTs, and its actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:57.520
the most overrated use case. NFTs have several&nbsp;
other use cases such as proof of ownership.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.320
You see, NFTs aren’t limited to just the&nbsp;
proving ownership of digital art. They can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:07.520
be used to prove the ownership of anything and a&nbsp;
great example is real estate. Imagine if you got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.000
an NFT based deed when you bought a house. You&nbsp;
could essentially create a record of the home’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.720
occupancy and ownership on the blockchain. And&nbsp;
when you’re ready to sell the house, you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.360
easily show potential buyers the entire history of&nbsp;
the home from the day it was built. Combine this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.520
with smart contracts and you can eliminate much&nbsp;
of the paperwork and tediousness associated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.720
real estate transactions. This same principle&nbsp;
applies to anything with substantial value.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:36.160
What if you got an NFT along with car purchases,&nbsp;
stock investments, bond investments, and so on and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:42.160
so forth. NFTs aren’t just limited to high value&nbsp;
items either. NFTs could also be implemented into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.120
everyday items like groceries and food. In this&nbsp;
scenario, NFTs would have less to do with proving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:52.400
ownership and more to do with keeping track of the&nbsp;
supply chain. After all, I don’t think the average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.720
person cares about have a digital certificate that&nbsp;
proves that they own the milk in their fridge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:01.760
However, if milk came with an NFT, people could&nbsp;
track down what farm it came from. While this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:06.640
is a cool idea, I bet the average person&nbsp;
would probably get over it within a week.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.440
Where this really makes sense is large companies&nbsp;
with complex supply chains. Companies like Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:16.160
and Tesla can leverage NFTs to maintain a detailed&nbsp;
log of the origins of their steel and lithium and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:21.040
aluminum and so on. And with all of the supply&nbsp;
chain problems we’ve been facing, the need for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:26.640
such a system is more evident than ever. Putting&nbsp;
the business world aside, NFTs also prove quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:31.440
valuable when it comes records and credentials.&nbsp;
What if birth certificates, medical records,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.920
passports, and drivers’ licenses were all securely&nbsp;
stored on the blockchain. You wouldn’t have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:40.400
worry about keeping all these documents safe or&nbsp;
lugging them around to hospitals and the DMV.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.040
A similar case could be made for credentials as&nbsp;
well. When you graduate college you could get an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:49.520
NFT that goes along with your diploma. This would&nbsp;
make it way easier for companies to verify your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:55.200
academic background. Similar NFTs could be handed&nbsp;
out electricians and plumbers and carpenters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:58.800
This way, when you hire a contractor, you&nbsp;
could ensure that they actually know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.320
they’re doing. All of these use cases are just&nbsp;
scratching the surface of NFTs. They could be used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:05.680
to validate tickets and patents and voting and so&nbsp;
much more. So, don’t be fooled by the apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.640
useless jpegs that are being circulated around&nbsp;
social media. The real applications of NFTs are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:16.720
far greater than apes and flexing.
THE NEEDED COLLAPSE:&nbsp;

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:21.840
While NFTs do have quite a bit of potential, the&nbsp;
current crash that we’re experiencing is well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:28.800
warranted and way overdue. As Elon Musk put it,&nbsp;
“it’s been raining money on fools for too long”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.760
and this is what’s given NFTs such a terrible&nbsp;
reputation in the first place. When people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:38.560
think of NFTs, they don’t think about how it could&nbsp;
revolutionize proof of ownership or how it could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:43.200
help create digital records and certifications.&nbsp;
Rather, they think about lavish celebrities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:48.080
dumping obscene amounts of money into pictures&nbsp;
that you can screenshot off of Google. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:52.480
as long as people are making millions flipping&nbsp;
jpegs, this will be the overwhelming narrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:57.200
surrounding NFTs. Not to mention, there’s&nbsp;
the whole scamming side of it as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:02.800
We’ve seen hundreds of fake NFT websites that&nbsp;
try to replicate legitimate NFT marketplaces. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:08.480
people end up spending tens of thousands on these&nbsp;
fake websites that don’t even get the real NFT.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:14.640
We’ve also seen the rise of a new breed of crypto&nbsp;
bots. These guys are now not only an expert at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:20.960
trading, but they’re also experts at picking out&nbsp;
the next big NFT. But, more than all this, likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:25.760
the most obvious sign of a massive bubble was&nbsp;
influencers creating their own NFTs and cashing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:30.320
out millions by shilling it to their audience.&nbsp;
And, the only way to cleanse all this garbage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:35.280
is for the entire sector to get obliterateda and&nbsp;
for people to get bored and leave. After that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:39.840
we can finally start to see true innovation once&nbsp;
again and unique applications that are actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:44.800
beneficial. Now, you might be saying that the&nbsp;
NFT market has already gotten crushed given that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:51.040
prices are down 80% across the board, but this is&nbsp;
just the beginning. You see, while overhyped NFTs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:55.520
have crashed in value relative to the dollar,&nbsp;
they haven’t crashed in value relative to crypto.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.520 --> 00:09:00.160
Take CryptoPunks for example. Each CryptoPunk&nbsp;
still costs 60 to 70 Ether just like it did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:05.760
in March and April. So, the entire crash so&nbsp;
far is solely due to crypto itself falling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:11.920
70 Ether used to be worth over $300,000,&nbsp;
but now it’s worth less than $100,000. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.240
popular NFTs like CryptoPunks and Bored Apes may&nbsp;
actually hold onto their value in relation to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:21.920
crypto decently well. But, the vast majority&nbsp;
of NFTs are likely headed towards 0, and as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:26.560
the crypto bear market continues, it just becomes&nbsp;
more and more likely that these NFTs start to lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:32.800
value against crypto as well. And while this will&nbsp;
no doubt be a painful journey for NFT hodlers, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:37.520
think it’s exactly what the market needs before it&nbsp;
moves on to the next phase of growth and adoption.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:42.080
THE FUTURE OF NFTS:&nbsp;

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:47.600
In the end, we live an extremely technology driven&nbsp;
world, but even now, parts of our daily lives that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:52.480
have yet to be digitalized. We still go through&nbsp;
mountains of paperwork when we transfer ownership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:57.440
and basic IDs like drivers’ licenses have yet&nbsp;
to be digitalized. Fortunately, it seems like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:02.240
NFTs are just what we need to digitalize these&nbsp;
last remaining portions of our lives. With that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:07.680
being said though, NFTs don’t have the best of&nbsp;
reputations amongst everyday people and its easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:12.240
to understand why. Everywhere on social media,&nbsp;
we’re constantly being exposed to degenerates who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:18.000
are gambling on random jpegs. And while some of&nbsp;
them are making money, the whole situation doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.960
exactly give you confidence and trust in the&nbsp;
market. Ideally, people would just put all this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:29.360
behind us and focus on the positive attributes&nbsp;
of NFTs, but this is never how it works out. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:34.160
every revolution, we see a bunch of garbage enter&nbsp;
the space and a massive bubble forming before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:39.120
a massive crash wipes out nearly everything. We&nbsp;
saw it with internet companies in the late 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:45.360
and we saw it with EVs, NFTs, 3D printing and who&nbsp;
knows what else over the past few years. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:50.480
we’re seeing all these sectors getting destroyed&nbsp;
and no one knows when exactly the pain will end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:56.960
But, eventually, we will make it to the other side&nbsp;
and we will see true innovation and growth. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:01.600
governments will start issuing NFT based IDs&nbsp;
and universities will start handing out NFT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:07.200
based diplomas. Who knows? Only time will tell,&nbsp;
but in the meantime, if you’re wondering what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:13.280
happened to NFTs? The answer is nothing other&nbsp;
than the cleansing that it so desperately needed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

